Z||000000||AusFiero||06-02-2001||04:45 AM||||As I don't have the exposure in Australia to all the Fiero mods and conversions around and can't find a lot on the net without hours of search. <BR>What is a Northstar ? <P>All I hae gathered so far from chat here is a kind of engine. Thought ok. Time to just ask about it and look stupid.<P>I am a body and interior man, no mechanic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||11
Z||000001||FieroGTguy||06-02-2001||05:18 AM||||I'm not sure what all the specs are, but a brief description is a V8 aluminum engine that is Cadiliac's top of the line engine.  Ideal for Fiero's because it is much lighter than small block chevies...  But you do add weight with the new tranny that you must use.  Other's can and will elaborate.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Greg||||reg||10
Z||000002||AusFiero||06-02-2001||05:26 AM||||cool...So I had worked out caddy V8 and alloy but wasn't sure.  Ok so how much horsepower has anyone got out of one and another question.<BR>How much does a good engine cost and who makes a kit to fit it?||||reg||1
Z||000003||TRiAD||06-02-2001||08:26 AM||||Well, it's an all-aluminum DOHC V8. It can only be used with an Auto, and the Caddy auto it came with works best. <BR>There are MANY ECU and OBD2 related problems that are still being worked out. They are rated at 275 and 300hp, depending on year and application. It was used in many Cadillac applications, and has a cousin in the Olds Aurora engine. <P>There's more, but that's all I know.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Hope that helps. <P>[edit]Oh, System1design.com does turn-key swaps for about $16K, the hardware alone would be about $12KUS. Shipping it all to Oz could be pricey. <BR>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Looking for a white '88 GT 5 sp...<BR>...Could it be...Found?<BR>Stay tuned!</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||AusFiero||06-02-2001||08:42 AM||||ouch..$12,000 for the parts. so about $23,000 plus about $1500 shipping.....a cut for the government and it is between $30,000 to $35,000. <P>350 Chev is starting to look the best option over here at about $10,000 to do it properly.||||reg||1
Z||000005||TRiAD||06-02-2001||09:17 AM||||That's how I'm going to go, and the price is much closer here. I just don't want to get into the whole ECU-gremlins thing with the Northstars. <BR>It's going to be LT1, LS1 (or Z06  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) for me!<P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Looking for a white '88 GT 5 sp...<BR>...Could it be...Found?<BR>Stay tuned!</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||06-02-2001||10:36 AM||||Hey dont feel so bad about being confused - I think Cadillac wanted to confuse people.<P> their ads on TV and in the rags referred to the new caddy blah blah blah... with the NorthStar System - only caddy has the NorthStar System.<P>   I saw these and thought, what the H$LL are they talking about? the car always points north?! It has a navigation system?!<P>  They did that on purpose so owners of older caddies would HAVE to run out to their dealers and find out what the NorthStar System is - where the salesguy would feed them a line of gobbelDeeGook and convince them if you dont have the NorthStar System in your Caddy, then you aint $hit!<P>   Rich people are so gullible. It worked. No self respecting Caddy owner would be caught dead in a car that doesnt have NorthStar<P>   translation: V8 engine.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SPDFREK||06-02-2001||10:59 AM||||ok, quick link as I am at work. <A HREF="http://www.cadillac.com/tech/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cadillac.com/tech/index.htm</A> <BR>hope this helps answer your question.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/4turbo.gif">      <BR>'88 Fiero 4cycl Turbo,<BR>'88 Fiero GT,<BR>'72 Nova 383 stroker||||reg||1
Z||000008||JSocha||06-02-2001||11:04 AM||||I believe the NS runs about 300HP (have to ask Dwight on Monday when I am back at work as he used to sell Cadillac's).<P>You can put quarters on the engine and they will not vibrate off, that is apparently how smooth running the motor is at idle.<P>Also, my folks have two cadillac's with the NorthStar motors.  If you blow a radiator or your water pump goes out, they can be driven I believe it is either at 55MPH or up to 55 miles without coolant or until you get to the next service station.<P>I was told yesterday that they cost around $8K US out of the crate for a replacement.  Was just up at Fisher Motor's in Minot where my Fiero currently is and asked them what it would cost for a NS motor if I was to order one for a project.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||TRiAD||06-02-2001||11:04 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>translation: V8 engine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uh, not quite. <BR>FYI only<P>The Northstar engine/system is the ONLY all-aluminum GM engine, their ONLY production DOHC V8 engine right now, and how many engines do YOU know that will shut down 4 of the cylinders and pump AIR through them to COOL the engine, if there's a coolant system failure?!<P>It is a technological system, it is advanced, it is confusing, but a regular old V8 lump, it isn't. <P><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Looking for a white '88 GT 5 sp...<BR>...Could it be...Found?<BR>Stay tuned!</I></B>||||reg||4
Z||000010||Formula88||06-02-2001||01:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B> Uh, not quite. <BR>FYI only<P>The Northstar engine/system is the ONLY all-aluminum GM engine, their ONLY production DOHC V8 engine right now, and how many engines do YOU know that will shut down 4 of the cylinders and pump AIR through them to COOL the engine, if there's a coolant system failure?!<P>It is a technological system, it is advanced, it is confusing, but a regular old V8 lump, it isn't. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The LS1 is all aluminum, unless you disqualify it for having a composite intake plenum/runners.<P>Still, the N* is GM's most technically advanced V8 powertrain system.  Like you said, a regular old lump V8 it isn't.  If someone ever gets the wiring scienced out for a swap - I may do one.  Right now, it's too much hassle (IMO).||||reg||1
Z||000011||TRiAD||06-02-2001||02:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> The LS1 is all aluminum, unless you disqualify it for having a composite intake plenum/runners.(IMO).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oops, my bad.  Thanks!<P>I wonder if a 4.0L Lexus V8 would fit.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Looking for a white '88 GT 5 sp...<BR>...Could it be...Found?<BR>Stay tuned!</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000012||thomas_l||06-02-2001||07:48 PM||||Don't feel bad about the Northstar. You made a remark about your wife's car (can't remember the make/model) and I had to do web searches all over just to see what one looked like  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Never seen one in the US. For that matter, those of us in North America: some time when you get bored check out the global car rental websites and look at the fleets in other countries. Thought it was pretty cool you could still get a Falcon in Australia...||||reg||10
Z||000013||AusFiero||06-02-2001||08:16 PM||||The old Falcon has come a long way too since Tickford have been involved with them. Not that I like Falcons much as they are really noisy compared to Commodores and chew through front tyres really quickly. Our best Falcon was the 1971 Ford Falcon GT HO Phase III. It was pretty much the fasted four door touring sedan in the world in its day.<P>Hey triad. Maybe great minds think alike. I was pndering that question last night actually and decided to look at other engine options.<P>Have found a cheap readily available source of engines that would probably go well in a Fiero.<P>Ok it will mean making it part rice but what the hell.<P>How does an all alloy  24 valve, quad cammed, twin turbocharged 3.2 ltr V8 sound as an engine option. We can get Japanese V8's really cheap in Australia seeing we are so close to Japan. The above option would cost about 2 thirds of a basic Chev V8 and weigh less than the original Fiero engine while upping the power to about 220KW. The best part in this country. Being so close to the original 2.8 engine in capacity it would not need any special checks or complainces to be registered. Is just considered a replacement engine.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>When I was young I spent 80% of my money on Women, Gambling and Alcohol. I guess I wasted the rest.||||reg||1
