Z||000015||Formula88||06-03-2001||01:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B> Oops, my bad.  Thanks!<P>I wonder if a 4.0L Lexus V8 would fit.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How about this engine:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1973-74_Lamborghini_Countach_4_litre_V12_engine_KRM.jpg"> <BR>Gimme a BIG shoehorn!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||thomas_l||06-03-2001||09:52 AM||||I'd say work with what you can get. Here in the US, the junkyards are full of old Caddys that can donate engines as well as engine control hardware. I guess the main benefit we see here is a GM - GM conversion, tending to make fit issues a <I>little</I> easier. With a non-GM swap you start getting into truly custom halfshaft/CV-joint combos, lots of fabricated mounts and linkage... wait that sounds almost like the small-block Chevy swap!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> If you can get your hands on a stout Asian V8 with an ECM that works you will definitely have a one of a kind setup. In fact, if it's similar to something available in the US it might mean another fabrication opportunity to go with your other stuff. No doubt this project would generate some commentary with respect to rice content hehehe - you would just need to design an appropriate sticker to go with it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000017||Ken Wittlief||06-03-2001||10:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B> Uh, not quite. <BR>FYI only<P>The Northstar engine/system is the ONLY all-aluminum GM engine, ...<BR>It is a technological system, it is advanced, it is confusing, but a regular old V8 lump, it isn't. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Stick a V8 in a black box and the N* in a black box and the inputs and the outputs still look the same to me - Air and fuel and throttle position goes in, exhaust and a spinning driveshaft comeout.<P>Yes it is advanced - yes it has features that a bonehead rich old lady who would keep driving any car even when the OIL light comes on might keep it from sef destructing (what happens if you drive it 56 miles BTW?!)<P>But comeon - its still an engine - its still a V8 motor. For them to hype it the way they did "only caddy has the N*" and not even tell you what they are talking about - is nothing but marketing BS.<P>remember back when the Infinity first came out? they had some guy in a quiet soothing voice doing ads for 6 months - showing video of cost lines and surf, and forests, and mountains and he went on and on "Infinity will be released soon - its such a revolution in transportation that we dont even consider it to be a car" and during that whole 6 month long ad campain they never showed a single picture of it, or gave any specs or info on it, so we didnt know that in the world the Infinity even was?<P>   and then when they had their big release, everyone looked at it and said, "Its a car! its just a stupid car!!!"<P>  I think the fact that the matching Ns tranny weighs so much pretty much trashes the whole Northstar SYSTEM concept - sure the engine is all alum - but you have to bolt their lead tranny to it, so in the end your car is heavier.<P>  To stop calling the V8 internal combustion engine in a car an 'engine' and start calling it a 'system' is nothing but marketing hype - it only confuses people (like the person that started this thread) and in the end it only alienates people who realize in the end what is going on.<P>   Caddy: "Its not an engine, its the Northstar System"<P>   Gearhead: "yeah right! Gimmy a break!"<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||TRiAD||06-03-2001||10:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR><B>How does an all alloy  24 valve, quad cammed, twin turbocharged 3.2 ltr V8 sound as an engine option?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uh, wow...just...wow...<P>If you build it...I will come (and see it). <BR>As for the Lambo V12 someone posted a pic of, I can get those fairly cheap (If you call under $10K cheap.) They're the older carbbed variety, but there is a source for a fe of them. You'd never fit it in without the logitudinal swap kit, and then, I would have better options to suggest. <P>Sounds awesome Aus!<BR><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com<BR>-----------------<BR>Looking for a white '88 GT 5 sp...<BR>...Could it be...Found?<BR>Stay tuned!</I></B>||||reg||1
